WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:01.950
<v Instructor>Next, what can we do,</v>

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.420
thanks to the File Gateway?

3
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.310
So here is some interesting thing to think

4
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.380
about when you're a solution architect.

5
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.030
So our traditional server can access

6
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.850
our File Gateway appliance on-premise using the NFS

7
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.320
or the SMB protocol.

8
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.930
And as we said, the files are going to be stored

9
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.480
in the backend into Amazon S3.

10
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.590
Now it is 100% for you possible to use a VPC,

11
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.900
and create another File Gateway appliance

12
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.820
and connect your E2 instances over with NFS and SMB.

13
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.870
Why would you do this?

14
00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.260
Well, maybe you want your EC 2 instances to access Amazon S3

15
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.510
as a file system and therefore you're going to use

16
00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.400
a File Gateway appliance for that.

17
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.980
So this could also be a way

18
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.350
for you to migrate your applications

19
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.620
from on-premise to the cloud using the same protocol

20
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.480
the same NFS or SMB protocol,

21
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.030
and it would be a first step to move your migration,

22
00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.070
your workloads into the cloud.

23
00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.230
But once the data is in Amazon S3,

24
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.260
remember every single file is going to be in Amazon S3.

25
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.750
Then you can use S3 events to send

26
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.280
that information to Lambda.

27
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.210
and then Lambda functions,

28
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.640
maybe process some of these objects for you.

29
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.240
Or we can use Athena to query all of our data in Amazon S3.

30
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.810
The beautiful thing is that Athena only uses the resources

31
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.600
of Amazon S3, and so it doesn't impact the performance

32
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.460
of our File Gateway.

33
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.930
We can use Redshift Spectrum

34
00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.240
or even EMR to analyze the data.

35
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.900
Finally, we are able to use cross region replication to,

36
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.310
for example, back up the data

37
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.160
into another region for disaster recovery.

38
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.920
So by using the File Gateway, it seems very simple.

39
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.160
We are exposing a network file system

40
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.510
to our corporate data center,

41
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.450
but because the data appears to be in S3 at the end,

42
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.700
it opens up so many different possibilities,

43
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.500
and I hope you see them.

44
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.170
Next we could have read only replicas.

45
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.020
So for example, you wanted to replicate a file system

46
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.510
from one on-premise data center to another.

47
00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.070
In the first on-premise data center,

48
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.590
you would create your File Gateway plans number one

49
00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.860
and your applications would write some files into it.

50
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.170
And then you would appear in an Amazon S3 bucket.

51
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.300
And in another on-premise data center,

52
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.250
you can create a read only replica,

53
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.370
so that your file system, your application servers

54
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.580
can read a read only copy of your File Gateway appliance

55
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.520
to get the data fast with low latency.

56
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.160
So this is yet another architecture you have to think about

57
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.190
when you're using your File Gateway appliance.

58
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.820
Next, there's way to do backup and lifecycle policies.

59
00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.290
So let's take an example.

60
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.500
Our file system, our application servers,

61
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.440
are talking to our file system on the File Gateway

62
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.890
and the files are going to be backed up into Amazon S3.

63
00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.710
But it is possible for these files to be used less and less.

64
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.290
And so we can define S3 lifecycle policies,

65
00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.820
maybe to migrate stuff to Amazon S3 Standard-IA first

66
00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.670
for infrequent access, and then after a while

67
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.770
migrate it to Amazon S3 Glacier.

68
00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.420
So what we've done is that effectively,

69
00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.750
we've made sure that we are getting some cost savings

70
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:59.460
for our file system on top of getting an NFS interface

71
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.350
for our application server on-premise.

72
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.600
So yet another thing to think about.

73
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.160
But this is Amazon S3,

74
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.440
so we have so many other possibilities.

75
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.030
The first one is that if you do enable

76
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.040
Amazon S3 ObjectVersioning,

77
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.170
then you can store multiple object versions

78
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.840
as they are modified, which is extremely helpful

79
00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.690
if one of your engineers, or one of your employees,

80
00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.850
is asking you to restore a specific file

81
00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.290
to a previous version.

82
00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.900
Because you're storing every single change in Amazon S3,

83
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.780
it is definitely possible for you to restore any file

84
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.020
to any previous version, or you can even restore

85
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.140
an entire file system to a previous version.

86
00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.723
But if you do so, to restore a file

87
00:03:35.723 --> 00:03:38.100
or a file system to a previous version,

88
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.860
you need to let your File Gateway appliance know

89
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.240
that there has been a change.

90
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.300
And so there is a cache, a RefreshCache API

91
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.880
on the gateway so that you notify the gateway

92
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.470
that there has been some restore happening,

93
00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:51.690
some changes happening on the Amazon S3 buckets,

94
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.580
and there will be a synchronization happening

95
00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.170
in the backend.

96
00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.230
Another one is to use Amazon S3 Object Lock.

97
00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.570
And this enables the File Gateway

98
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.700
to be a Write Once Read Many,

99
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.640
so WORM data network file system.

100
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.650
So the idea is that if there are any modifications

101
00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.600
or renames in the file share clients,

102
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.490
the file gateway will create a new version

103
00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.100
of the object without affecting prior versions

104
00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.250
and the original locked version will remain unchanged.

105
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:18.990
Effectively, the giving, making sure

106
00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:20.730
that you're not losing any data

107
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.340
and that the original data will never be deleted.

108
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.100
And so for compliance reasons, or audits,

109
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.220
or restore again versions, then reasons this could be

110
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.390
really, really helpful.

111
00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.500
So that's it for this lecture.

112
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.450
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

